Q: What is the alternative to declare a Variable-Length array in C, that may exceed Stack size, without the need of Dynamic Memory allocation? A: <p>If you have lot of calls with small N, which you know will fit into memory, you can put conditional logic, where the code will conditionally use stack or mmap.</p>
<p>This is based on ideas in Medium article: <a href="https://medium.com/@yair.lenga/avoiding-malloc-for-small-strings-in-c-with-variable-length-arrays-vlas-7b1fbcae7193" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Avoiding malloc for Small Strings in C With Variable Length Arrays (VLAs)</a></p>
<pre><code>size_t need_bytes = ...
bool use_mmap = need_bytes &gt; 500*1000 ; // Example value
int n_vla = use_mmap ? 1 : n ;          // set to 1, if allocating with mmap
void *base = use_mmap ? mmap(NULL, need_bytes, PROT_WRITE | PROT_READ, MAP_ANONYMOUS, 0, 0) : NULL ;
char *p = base ;

// Conditional allocation/use of VLA
double Z_space[3][n_vla] ;
double (*Z)[3][n] = (void *) (use_mmap) ? ( p += sizeof(*Z) ) : Z_space ;
</code></pre>
<p>You can probably make code more compact with a macro</p>

